4.2. Perceptual modalities
It is apparent that we can sometimes divide attention between the eye and ear better than between two auditory channels or two visual channels. That is, cross-modal time-sharing is better than intra-modal time-sharing. As examples, Wickens et al. (1983) found advantages to cross-modal over intra-modal displays in both a laboratory tracking experiment and in a fairly complex  ̄ight simulation. Parkes and Coleman (1990) found that discrete route guidance was better presented auditorily than visually while subjects were concurrently driving a simulated vehicle
